**Ties:** in "aaabdaaabac" after the first merge (aa becomes 256), the pairs (256, 97) and (97, 98) both occur twice. `max` over a Counter picks the first seen; either choice yields the final ids [258, 100, 258, 97, 99] here, but in general the merge table depends on the tie rule. **Encoding order:** applying merges in learned order is what reproduces training segmentation; minbpe's encoder repeatedly picks the pair with the lowest merge index. **Pre tokenisation:** GPT-2 and GPT-4 tokenizers first split text with a regex (letters, digits, punctuation, leading spaces) so merges never cross those boundaries. **Decoding** can produce invalid UTF-8 if a sequence ends mid character; decode with `errors="replace"`. Karpathy's tokenizer lecture traces many LLM quirks (arithmetic, trailing spaces, non English cost) back to these choices.
